<Step ID="7" Task="Let n be the smallest natural number greater than L that is not in G. For this n, what are the possible positions m = n - s for s in S with s ≤ n?" Difficulty="4" Token="40" Rely="3,6"/>
<Step ID="8" Task="Using the choice of n from Step 7 and the definition of G from Step 3, show whether any m = n - s is a losing position (i.e., in Λ)?" Difficulty="6" Token="60" Rely="7"/>
<Step ID="9" Task="Based on the analysis in Step 8 and the recursive definition from Step 2, combined with the assumption that all positions between L+1 and n-1 are winning (since they are in G), what is the status of position n (winning or losing)?" Difficulty="7" Token="70" Rely="2,8"/>
<Step ID="10" Task="How does the conclusion about n from Step 9 contradict the assumption of finite losing positions from Step 3, thereby proving there are infinitely many initial situations where the second player wins?" Difficulty="5" Token="50" Rely="9"/>
</Plan>
